("Why do we see lightning before thunder?","Light travels about 3 × 10⁸ m/s, but sound only about 340 m/s, so the flash reaches us first. Count the seconds between them and divide by 3 to get the distance in kilometres.",["lightning before thunder","why lightning first then thunder"]),
("Why does ice float on water?","Ice is less dense than liquid water: when water freezes, its molecules lock into an open hexagonal pattern held by hydrogen bonds, taking up about 9% more space.",["why ice floats","ice floats on water why"]),
("Why do stars twinkle?","Stars twinkle because their light passes through moving layers of air with different temperatures, which bend the light slightly in changing directions. Planets twinkle less because they appear as tiny discs, not points.",["why stars twinkle","twinkling of stars"]),
("Why is the sea salty?","Rain slowly dissolves minerals from rocks, and rivers carry them to the sea. When seawater evaporates, the salts stay behind, so over millions of years the sea became salty (about 3.5% salt).",["why sea water is salty","why is ocean salty"]),
("Why do leaves change colour?","Leaves are green because of chlorophyll. In autumn, trees stop making it, so the hidden yellow and orange pigments (carotenoids) show, and some trees make red pigments (anthocyanins).",["why leaves change color","autumn leaves colour"]),
("Why do we yawn?","Scientists aren't fully sure. A leading idea is that yawning helps cool the brain; it happens when we're tired or bored, and it's contagious — seeing someone yawn makes us yawn.",["why we yawn","why do people yawn"]),
("Why do onions make us cry?","Cutting an onion releases a gas (syn-propanethial-S-oxide) that irritates the eyes, so they make tears to wash it away. Chilling the onion or using a sharp knife helps.",["why onions make you cry","onion tears"]),
("Why is the Earth round?","Gravity pulls equally towards the centre from all directions, so large bodies like planets squeeze into a ball shape. Earth is slightly flattened at the poles because it spins.",["why earth is round","shape of earth"]),
("Why do we have leap years?","Earth takes about 365.24 days to go around the Sun. Adding a day (29 February) every 4 years keeps the calendar in step with the seasons; years divisible by 100 but not 400 skip it.",["reason for leap year","why february has 29 days"]),
("Why is blood red?","Blood is red because of haemoglobin, an iron-containing protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Oxygen-rich blood is bright red; oxygen-poor blood is darker red.",["why blood is red","colour of blood"]),
("Why do we need to sleep?","Sleep lets the body repair itself, the brain store memories and clear waste, and hormones balance. Without enough sleep, attention, mood and immunity all suffer.",["why we need sleep","importance of sleep"]),
("Why does a spoon look bent in water?","Light bends (refracts) when it passes from water into air because it travels at different speeds in them, so the part of the spoon under water appears in a different place.",["spoon looks bent in water","pencil looks bent in water","refraction example"]),
("Why do birds migrate?","Birds migrate to find food, warmer weather and safe places to breed. They navigate using the Sun, stars, Earth's magnetic field and landmarks. Arctic terns fly the longest migrations of any bird.",["why birds migrate","bird migration"]),
("Why do cats purr?","Cats purr by vibrating the muscles of their voice box, usually when content, but also to calm themselves when hurt or stressed.",["why cats purr"]),
("Why is the grass green?","Grass is green because of chlorophyll, which absorbs red and blue light for photosynthesis and reflects green light to our eyes.",["why plants are green","why leaves are green"]),
("Why do we feel hungry?","When your stomach is empty and blood sugar drops, the stomach releases the hormone ghrelin, which tells the brain it's time to eat. After eating, leptin and other signals say you're full.",["why we feel hungry","hunger hormone"]),
("Why do we get goosebumps?","Goosebumps happen when tiny muscles at the base of hairs contract from cold or strong emotions. In furry ancestors this made fur stand up for warmth or to look bigger.",["goosebumps","why do we get goosebumps"]),
("Why does the Moon change shape?","The Moon doesn't really change: we see different amounts of its sunlit half as it orbits Earth every 29.5 days, giving the phases new moon, crescent, half moon, gibbous and full moon.",["phases of the moon","why does moon change shape","moon phases"]),
("Why is the Dead Sea called dead?","The Dead Sea is so salty (about 34%) that fish and plants can't live in it, and people float easily. It's also the lowest land point on Earth, about 430 m below sea level.",["dead sea","why can you float in the dead sea"]),
("Why do we sweat?","Sweating cools the body: sweat evaporates from the skin and takes heat away with it.",["why we sweat","why do we perspire"]),
("Why do our fingers wrinkle in water?","Fingers wrinkle after soaking because nerves tighten blood vessels under the skin; scientists think the wrinkles help grip wet things, like tyre treads.",["wrinkly fingers in water","why fingers wrinkle"]),
("Why is the sunset orange?","At sunset, sunlight passes through much more air, which scatters away the blue light, leaving mostly orange and red to reach our eyes.",["why sunset is orange","orange sky evening"]),
("Why do metals feel cold?","Metals feel colder than wood at the same temperature because they conduct heat away from your hand much faster.",["why metal feels cold","why does metal feel cold"]),
("Why does bread rise?","Yeast in dough eats sugars and releases carbon dioxide gas, which gets trapped in the stretchy gluten and makes the bread rise. Baking soda does the same job quickly in cakes.",["why bread rises","how does yeast work"]),
("Why do we hiccup?","Hiccups are sudden spasms of the diaphragm, followed by the vocal cords snapping shut ('hic'). Eating fast, fizzy drinks or excitement can trigger them; they usually stop on their own.",["hiccups","why do we hiccup","how to stop hiccups"]),
